awns early.\n\nReason:  \nN: g8-f6 develops a knight to a natural square, controls key central squares (e4 and d5), prepares for castling, and doesn’t commit pawns unnecessarily. It avoids passivity and fights for central dominance. While d7-d5 is tempting, playing ...d5 now would be committal and might lead to structural weaknesses if not timed well. Nf6 is flexible and strong.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6'}
